text:
ingrid: Quick follow-up to the allocator RFC — idea: a "lazy-span-reuse" layer that prefers reusing recently-returned host spans for small allocations instead of immediately splitting or spilling to host. Trying to capture tradeoffs and a minimal API.

danielle: high level: why prefer reuse? isn't freeing asap better for fragmentation?

ingrid: Two main goals: 1) avoid unnecessary NVMe/host alloc churn for workloads with fleeting hotspots (same tenant spikes), 2) keep warm mappings to reduce tail latency on re-materialization. But yes it trades longer-lived holes vs. potential fragmentation.

marco: Could you define lifecycle? e.g., when span returns to host it's put in a reuse bucket with TTL?

ingrid: Exactly. On host-offload callback we mark span as "recent_return" and place it in a size-bucketed LRU with a small reuse_window (configurable: e.g., 200-1000ms). Alloc path checks bucket before splitting or remote-alloc. If found and still backed, prefer reuse.

jiyu: How do we ensure correctness if span was partially reclaimed by background GC/compaction or tenant eviction?

ingrid: Reuse only applies to spans that are entirely free and not pinned. We keep a version counter in span header; allocation does CAS on header to claim. If mismatch, fallthrough. No live-migration.

marco: Hmm CAS on header is fine but heavy for hot paths. Any way to batch-check?

ingrid: Plan: fast-path: per-core cache of recently-seen buckets; probablistic check of a small bitmap in span header (atomic load). CAS only on the slow confirm path. That keeps common-case cheap.

danielle: Operational concerns: this may increase resident set on host if we bias reuse. How do we avoid holding onto large free slabs that block large allocations?

ingrid: Two mitigations: 1) size-bucket eviction policy — larger span requests can evict small reuse entries; 2) hard upper bound per-pool reuse-reservoir (% of pool). And reuse_window decays adaptively if allocation failures rise.

jiyu: Telemetry we should capture: reuse-hits, reuse-misses (expired), spill-after-reuse, reservoir-occupancy, TTL-adjust events. Also signal for dedicated/private customers to toggle.

ops-bot: reuse-sampler job scheduled, will emit metrics to telemetry/reuse.* every 10s :satellite:

marco: Interaction with current prefetcher: if we reuse a span that previously had prefetched K/V, its warmness is beneficial. But if reuse holds warm spans too long it may starve compaction.

ingrid: Right — we want a warmness score. Proposal: only keep spans with warmness > threshold in reuse buckets. Warmness derived from recent access counters or last-touch timestamp. That biases toward actually useful spans.

danielle: Edge-case: multi-tenant machines — reuse of spans could leak cross-tenant locality signals or cause unfairness. Make reuse reservoir per-tenant or per-queue?

ingrid: We'll keep reuse per-tenant when tenant id exists, else per-pool. For Dedicated nodes we default to off. For Private, on/off configurable.

marco: API sketch?

ingrid: Draft API (C-ish):

/* called when a span is evicted to host */
void on_span_return(span_id s) {
  if (span.warmness < WARM_THRESH) return;
  pool->reuse_bucket[sizeclass(s)].insert_lru(s, now + reuse_window);
}

/* allocation path */
span_id try_reuse(size_t bytes) {
  bucket = pool->reuse_bucket[sizeclass(bytes)];
  candidate = bucket.peek_lru();
  if (!candidate) return NULL;
  if (candidate.expired() || !candidate.backed()) { bucket.pop(); return NULL; }
  if (atomic_cas_span_header(candidate, FREE -> ALLOC)) return candidate;
  // contention/failure -> miss, fall through
  return NULL;
}

/* adaptive controller */
void reuse_controller_tick(pool) {
  if (alloc_failures_high()) pool->reuse_window = max(min_window, pool->reuse_window * 0.8);
  if (tail_lat_ok()) pool->reuse_window = min(max_window, pool->reuse_window * 1.05);
}

marco: Nice. What about correctness with external tools that inspect allocations? Need to ensure span metadata remains authoritative.

ingrid: Yes we won't change allocator invariants. Reuse is an optimization on top of existing free lists; if any external inspector touches low-level metadata they'd see spans as free until claimed. We'll update diagnostic views to show reuse state.

danielle: Performance testing plan? microbench with bursty inference workloads (TinyChat sim): measure alloc latency, tail query latency, host-alloc ops, and fragmentation delta. Also long-run to see reservoir effects.

ingrid: I'll add unit perf harness and two stage CI: microbench (latency + reuse metrics) and long soak (10k requests) comparing reuse on/off.

jiyu: I'm concerned about fragmentation increase. Can we couple reuse with opportunistic background coalescing (without moving live pages) so fragmentation is reversible?

ingrid: Yes we can combine: reuse prefers short TTLs and background merges free spans when reuse entries expire. Different from in-place compaction because we still won't migrate live objects.

marco: LGTM. One more: prefer reuse only for spans <= X bytes by default; avoid holding big spans.

ingrid: Agreed. Default max_reuse_span = 256KB. Configurable per-pool.

ops-bot: feature-flag created: runtime.reuse_span.enabled -> default true (hosted), false (dedicated)

ingrid: I'll draft RFC follow-up PR with API stubs, telemetry list, simple controller, and perf plan. Will share benchmarks. Any objections?

danielle: No objections. Please include knobs and opt-out.

marco: +1

jiyu: +1 — add a stress test that simulates agressive multi-tenant churn.

ingrid: Will do. Thanks all.
